            <ArticleTitle>[Health-related quality of life across weight categories in childhood].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>170-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.gaceta.2011.07.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze differences in health-related quality of life across weight categories based on body mass index in boys and girls aged 8 to 12 years old.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional study with 678 schoolchildren aged between 8 and 12 years from the province of Alicante (Spain) was conducted. The scores on the dimensions of health-related quality of life, assessed by the Spanish version of the Child Health and Illness Profile-Child Edition, across the weight categories (according to the World Health Organization Child Growth Standards) and sex, were compared.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Overweight or obesity was found in 28.2% and 15.3% of the sample, respectively. There were no statistically significant differences (p &gt; 0.05) across the weight categories on the distinct dimensions of health-related quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Children are unaware of the impact of obesity on quality of life. Interventions to increase knowledge of this issue are required.</AbstractText>
